cific person or company. Regarding name and gameplay of <i>Tank Wars: Anniversary Edition</i> we want to mention Kenneth Morse at least. Anyway, many games, clones, adaptations, and remakes following this basic concept have been made through the years. Some newer games have a lot of different game settings, modes, and other features as well as modern graphics and effects. However, <i>Tank Wars: Anniversary Edition</i>, as a remake of an adaptation from the year 1997, deliberately distances itself from fancy newfangled developments. Nevertheless, we don't want to bore you or hurt your eyes and ears more than necessary, so we tried to walk a tightrope between pure old school, pixelated, low-resolution 2D computer graphics and some modern-ish concepts and effects.<br><br>So, <i>Tank Wars: Anniversary Edition</i> is not intended to be a one-to-one recreation of any previous <i>Tank Wars</i> title. Instead, it is the modern remake of my own 1997 adaptation, which itself was mainly inspired by Kenneth Morse's original 1986 game. Over several decades of regular play sessions with friends, mechanics were refined, weapons were added, removed, or redesigned, and new ideas found their way into the game. <i>Anniversary Edition</i> represents the latest evolution of that journey.<br><br>Interested in the history behind the game, the origins of the project, the story behind the online mode, future development, bug reporting, and feature requests? Check out the pinned Community post: &quot;<strong>Welcome to Tank Wars: Anniversary Edition</strong>&quot;.",
